ht_test.cljc:1268:          _ (reset! (:refuse w) (constantly true))\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1277:                      (run-world w 50 (+ (:now w) 500) (constantly false)\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1281:          w (run-world w 100 (+ (:now w) 60000) (constantly false) check)]\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1388:     _ (reset! (:refuse w) (constantly true))\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1533:          w (run-world w 50 (+ (:now w) 20000) (constantly false))\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1565:          w (run-world w 50 (+ (:now w) 20000) (constantly false))]\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1611:    (let [w (run-world frozen 50 (+ (:now frozen) 2000) (constantly false))]\n./test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:1776:     refuse (atom (constantly true))\n./orchestrator-logs/1793203595-jolt-j4d-j5-orchestration.md:195:    figure (181,088 B / 3,312 allocs — constant).\n./public/chp/blog/datalog-compiler-infrastructure.blog:65:            \"Source Code\\n    ↓\\n  Parse → AST (in-memory tree)\\n    ↓\\n  Type Check (walk tree, annotate types)\\n    ↓\\n  Optimize (constant folding, dead code elimination)\\n    ↓\\n  Code Generation (emit bytecode/assembly)\\n    ↓\\n  Output\"]]\n./public/chp/blog/datalog-compiler-infrastructure.blog:291:            \" — Which variables have constant values? (iterate until stable)\"]\n./public/chp/blog/datalog-compiler-infrastructure.blog:428:            \"[:find ?call ?loop ?optimization\\n :where\\n ;; Find a function call\\n [?call :ast/type :application]\\n [?call :ast/operator ?fn]\\n\\n ;; The function is pure (analysis 1: purity)\\n [?fn :purity/pure true]\\n\\n ;; Call is inside a loop\\n [?loop :ast/type :loop]\\n [?call :ast/ancestor ?loop]\\n\\n ;; Loop is a hot path (analysis 2: profiling)\\n [?loop :profiling/hot-path true]\\n\\n ;; Arguments are loop-invariant (analysis 3: data flow)\\n [?call :data-flow/constant-args true]\\n\\n ;; → Optimization opportunity: Hoist call outside loop\\n [(vector :hoist-invariant ?call ?loop) ?optimization]]\"]]\n./test/resources/dao/space/datomic-q-v1.errata.md:336:## E14 — Syntax depth is bounded by a documented constant\n./test/resources/dao/space/datomic-q-v1.errata.md:343:queries are bounded by the same constant in the scanner. Source descriptor\n./public/chp/blog/beyond-ffi-yin-vm-datom-streaming.blog:58:           \"constant \\\"stopping and starting\\\" of the execution flow creates unacceptable latency.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/why-object-databases-failed.blog:46:           \"Developers refactor their object models constantly. Every little refactor becomes a schema migration in an OODB.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/the-way-of-babashka.blog:140:            \"(def constant-folding-rules\\n  '[[(constant-foldable ?node ?result)\\n     [?node :node/type :call]\\n     [?node :node/fn ?op]\\n     [(contains? #{+ - * /} ?op)]\\n     [?node :node/args ?args]\\n     (all-literal ?args)\\n     (compute ?op ?args ?result)]])\"]]\n./docs/datomic.md:165:`get` and `delete` genuinely take **two** arguments (confirmed: `get(Object, Object)` / `delete(Object, Object)` in the bytecode), and both trailing parameters are named `consistent?` in the arglists. The trailing argument's role is recovered from the DynamoDB backend: it is a **consistent-read flag**. `KVDynamo.get` branches on it and, when truthy, adds `:consistentRead` to the `datomic.ddb/get-item` request (the keyword is visible in the class's constant pool and static initializer). `KVMem` ignores it (bytecode references only `this.m` and the key) and `KVSql` binds only the key in its `WHERE id = ?` — both are read-your-writes by construction. This is how the \"strong consistency for `meta` keys\" requirement below is actually implemented: per-read, on the backends that need it, not per-store.\n./docs/datomic.md:239:`KVMem` realizes this in `put-when` (`datomic/kv_mem/KVMem$put_when__30075.class`, constant pool: `select-keys`, `keys`, `ensure`, `dissoc`, `ok`) — the `:ensure` map is compared against the stored entry and then `dissoc`ed before storing, so it never persists. `KVSql` has the same shape at the SQL level, which is why §2 emits two distinct statements: a plain `insert` for the create case and the rev-gated `update datomic_kvs set rev=?, map=?, val=? where id=? and rev=?` for the overwrite case, where zero rows updated means the write lost the race.\n./docs/datomic.md:341:* **The conditional write folded into `put`.** The `put-when` helper creates unconditionally on an absent key, and on a present key writes only if `:ensure` carries a `:rev` matching the stored one (plus any additional `:ensure` fields), then strips `:ensure` before storing. Its constant pool (`select-keys`, `keys`, `ensure`, `dissoc`, `ok`) spells out the comparison; `ConcurrentMap.replace` (or `compare-and-set!` on an atom) supplies the atomicity. This is the in-memory analog of `... WHERE id=? AND rev=?`.\n./docs/datomic.md:426:* **A log must totally order appends.** Its public arity is `(append [log {:keys [t next-t] :as header} body])` — the *caller* supplies `t`, so the backend must reject an append at a `t` already taken. Otherwise two writers claim the same position and one transaction vanishes with no error raised. That is a conditional write, and the DynamoDB backend's constant pool contains exactly that: `conditional-put-request`. It is the only conditional-write vocabulary anywhere in the `core2` tree.\n./docs/datomic.md:462:This is the literal DDL `datomic.sql` emits (recovered from the class constant pool), not a reconstruction — hence lowercase, `varchar` rather than `TEXT`, and an inline primary key:\n./docs/datomic.md:476:The `KVStore` operations from §1 map directly onto SQL, all keyed by the single `id` column. These are the literal statements emitted by `datomic.sql`, quoted as they appear in the constant pool:\n./public/chp/blog/semantic-bytecode-benchmarks.blog:80:           [:li \"Building the constant pool with keyword references\"]\n./public/chp/blog/shibi-as-money.blog:71:            \"The network needs enough paths that payments don't constantly fail\"]]\n./public/chp/blog/shibi-as-money.blog:86:           \"Ripple-style credit, by contrast, requires dense trust connections just to function. If trust paths don't exist, payments fail. ShiBi deliberately avoids that dependency because it would create a bottleneck. Most pairs of agents would not have direct or second-degree trust connections, so Ripple-style payments would constantly stall.\"]\n./src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:864:   against Rule R. A fresh-code producer supplies the current constant.\"\n./docs/orchestrator-log.md:1622:composition, cadence and deadline are named options rather than constants of\n./docs/orchestrator-log.md:7209:  with four NEW findings (P1: AST definition query missed constant-key\n./docs/orchestrator-log.md:11473:Phase 8 (Fable's plan: `collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md`; slices 8ab, 8c, 8d, 8e, 8f, 8g): 8ab and 8d are implemented by codex and pushed. 8ab: both descriptors lose `:dim/lift-to` and declare `:hash :blake3`; the six surviving hash sites (`debruijn_code`, `debruijn_register_code`, `debruijn`) use `jing/blake3`; 49 distinct constants regenerated and reproducible (`collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj`, byte-identical twice); descriptor diff vs HEAD is exactly the two rows; no A address moved. 8d: 16 old-codec test files (141 deftests), `lift_support`, `v2_support`, `checkpoint_fixtures`, three fixtures and the scalars minter removed; surviving helpers moved to `rows_support`; `handoff.cljc` (3783 lines) and `checkpoint.cljc` are RETAINED until 8f because `test/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:241` (`cell-refusal`, an other-seat C4 file, untouched) still calls `handoff/export-task` and cannot be ported without editing the walker/old-kernel harness; codex also made one trivial port in `test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc` (`an-image-carries-no-profile-test` now uses a semantic-register machine, a for