t\\n   (order is irrelevant: the result is a set); nil or an empty one yields\\n   no binding, and anything else, a map included, is a query error.\\\"\\n  [binding out ret clause]\\n  (if-not (vector? out)\\n    (if-let [b (unify binding out ret)] [b] [])\\n    (let [kind (classify-in-pattern out)\\n          terms (case kind\\n                  :coll (butlast out)\\n                  :relation (first out)\\n                  :tuple out)]\\n      (when (or (and (= :coll kind) (not= 2 (count out)))\\n                (and (= :relation kind) (not= 1 (count out)))\\n                (some #(or (= '... %) (vector? %)) terms))\\n        (throw (ex-info\\n                 \\\"Unsupported binding form — use ?x, [?a ?b], [?x ...] or [[?a ?b]]\\\"\\n                 {:binding-form out, :clause clause})))\\n      (when (and (#{:coll :relation} kind)\\n                 (not (or (nil? ret) (sequential? ret) (set? ret))))\\n        (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Function return for a \\\"\\n                             (if (= :coll kind) \\\"collection\\\" \\\"relation\\\")\\n                             \\\" binding must be nil, sequential or a set\\\")\\n                        {:binding-form out, :returned ret, :clause clause})))\\n      (case kind\\n        :tuple (if-let [b (unify-tuple binding out ret clause)] [b] [])\\n        :coll (let [term (first out)]\\n                (into [] (keep #(unify binding term %)) ret))\\n        :relation (into [] (keep #(unify-tuple binding terms % clause)) ret)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- eval-fn-clause\\n  [clause binding ctx]\\n  (let [[fsym-and-args & result-vars] clause\\n        fsym (first fsym-and-args)\\n        args (rest fsym-and-args)\\n        special (eval-special-fn fsym args binding)]\\n    (if special\\n      (let [{:keys [ret]} special]\\n        (when (> (count result-vars) 1)\\n          (throw\\n            (ex-info\\n              \\\"Function clause takes one binding form; use a tuple [?a ?b] for multi-return\\\"\\n              {:clause clause})))\\n        (if (empty? result-vars)\\n2:description: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\\n7:Canonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\\n18:backed by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\\n19:security-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\\n33:| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\\n57:|                 |                                        | (Architect & security sign-offs). Do not assign general implementation or routine reviews.                   |\\n61:| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Reserved strictly for Architectural Review roles. Do not assign implementation tasks.   |\\n80:| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\\n82:|                             | **Best for:** Architecture definition, security sign-offs.         | one-shot implementations where strict rigor is overkill.            |\\n84:| `claude-opus-5.5`           | Performs at Fable 5.1 levels for most work at markedly lower cost  | Still higher latency/cost than Sonnet 5. **Avoid for:** Fast QA     |\\n90:|                             | high cost-efficiency. **Best for:** Core implementation, deep QA   | architectural sign-offs on high-risk cryptographic or consensus     |\\n91:|                             | adversarial code reviews, multi-file refactors, and test suites.   | boundaries where Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 / Astra is mandated.          |\\n98:|                             | workflows and adversarial reviews.                                 | relative to size. **Avoid for:** General use (use Flash instead).   |\\n104:| `gemini-4-argon`            | Frontier reasoning with configurable effort and deep synthesis     | Higher latency/cost at medium/high effort than Flash-tier models;   |\\n107:|                             | **Best for:** System architecture, design/code review, complex     | headless tasks requiring in-session JVM test execution.             |\\n108:|                             | debugging, and multi-step implementation.                          |                                                                     |\\n123:| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\\n125:|                             | Capability includes implementation, but the current codex policy   | unsuitable for implementation.                                      |\\n128:| `gpt-6-luna`                | Most affordable GPT-6 model; optimized for high-volume tasks and   | Low-cost does not mean suitable for unbounded design or the hardest |\\n132:| `gpt-6.1-sol`               | Intelligent everyday workhorse with near-Astra intelligence, 1M+   | More expensive than Luna. Use Astra when the task is the hardest    |\\n135:|                             | predecessor. **Best for:** Complex implementation, advanced coding,|                                                                     |\\n136:|                             | high-stakes security, and architectural review.                    |                                                                     |\\n138:| `gpt-6-terra`               | Balanced intelligence and cost. **Best for:** Default production   | Use Sol or Astra for the hardest reasoning, high-risk security, or  |\\n140:|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\\n171:|                             | scale reviews.                                                     | searches or quick edits.                                            |\\n182:Every change must be reviewed by a model from a **different family** to ensure\\n183:independent defect discovery. Never silently substitute a same-family reviewer.\\n186:Any author from one family must be reviewed by a model from a different family.\\n188:Routine review applies only when independent; architectural or security review\\n2:description: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n12:bb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\n13:bb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n14:# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\n15:bb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\n16:bb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\n17:bb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\n18:bb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\n19:bb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n20:bb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n21:bb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\n22:bb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n23:bb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n24:# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n31:# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n45:# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n47:# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n49:# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n50:# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n57:# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n59:# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\n63:# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n65:# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\n73:### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n75:`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\n84:needed builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n85:`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\n95:### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n97:`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\n102:bb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\n103:bb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\n104:bb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\n105:bb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\n106:bb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\n107:bb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\n121:and lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\n126:1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n127:2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n128:3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n130:5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n157:clj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n160:clj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n163:The Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n164:[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\n168:`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\n170:each of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\n171:script compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n172:<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n175:under `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\n179:`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\n182:stale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\n190:- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n193:- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n236:shards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\n258:  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./archive/dao-space-query/1793093377000-architect-datomic-q-phase5-udivergence.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '206,260p' test/resources/dao/space/datomic-q-v1.errata.md; sed -n '323,354p' docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md; cat docker/oracle/reconcile/probe_aggregates.clj; cat docker/oracle/reconcile/idmap_rerun.clj\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## E15 — Phase 2 aggregate conventions and their limits\\n\\nFrozen DaoSpace-side conventions (Client behavior is unmeasured; every case\\nis `oracle` pending):\\n\\n- Aggregates fold the content-distinct rows projected to the `:find`,\\n  `:with` and aggregate-argument variables, so duplicates vanish unless\\n  `:with` distinguishes them; `distinct` returns a set.\\n- `median`: odd count -> the middle element itself (its kind kept); even\\n  count -> the double mean of the two middle elements. `variance`:\\n  POPULATION variance (divide by n); a single value gives 0.0; `stddev` is its\\n  square root. `(min n ?x)` -> the n smallest ascending, `(max n ?x)` -> the n\\n  largest descending, duplicates kept, n above the count returns everything.\\n- Order statistics sort by exact numeric order across kinds with the owner's\\n  tie rule (an exact operand before a float64, then the shorter canonical\\n  encoding), so tied kinds order identically on every host; non-numeric\\n  operands are refused (\\\"requires numeric operands\\\"), never coerced.\\n- Float results are asserted for EXACT equality with no tolerance: values are\\n  folded as doubles in the fixed sorted order, and `+ - * /` and `sqrt` are\\n  correctly rounded IEEE-754 operations on every host. This is claimed only\\n  for the integer inputs the cases use, not for arbitrary floats.\\n- The kind of an INTEGRAL double result (7.0) is not host-portable: a\\n  JavaScript number cannot be told from the integer 7 without a carrier, so\\n  the corpus compares such results by value (`numeric-value`); the JVM kind\\n  is pinned in `dao.space.query-aggregates-test`. The tie ORDER of kinds is\\n  likewise pinned there, with `cbor/content=`, not in this harness.\\n- A `distinct` set collapses elements the host treats as `=`: on the JVM\\n  `1.0M` and `1.00M` are one member (the E1 limitation applied to a set).\\n- Numeric carriers (decimals, ratios, float64 carriers) have no host\\n  arithmetic on JavaScript and Dart: `variance`, `stddev` and an even-count\\n  `median` refuse explicitly (\\\"does not accept a numeric carrier\\\",\\n  `agg.variance-carrier-refused`, hosts `cljs` and `cljd`). The JVM evaluates\\n  BigDecimal as a host number. Order statistics (odd `median`, `min`/`max`\\n  with n) never do arithmetic and work with carriers everywhere. A float64\\n  is a carrier on JavaScript only; a Dart double and a JVM Double are native\\n  numbers. Decimals are carriers on both JavaScript and Dart. So an\\n  even-count `median` over tied kinds is evaluated on `clj` and `cljd`\\n  (`agg.median-tied-kinds`), refused explicitly on `cljs`\\n  (`agg.median-tied-kinds-carrier-refused`), and the odd-count\\n  `agg.median-tied-kinds-odd` (no arithmetic) runs on every host.\\n- `rand` and `sample` remain excluded, at both layers: the core throws\\n  \\\"Unknown aggregate in :find\\\", and the Datomic adapter refuses them first as\\n  `:unsupported` (\\\"randomized aggregates are excluded from the profile\\\").\\n- `(max n ?x)` tie order: among values that tie numerically the order is the\\n  reverse of the ascending order, so a float64 precedes the exact operand\\n  (`[2.0 1.0 1]`, where `(min n ?x)` gives `[1 1.0 2.0]`).\\n- An aggregate argument must be a query variable: `(count [1 2])` and other\\n  non-variable arguments are refused (\\\"aggregate argument must be a query\\n  variable\\\"). Earlier phases parsed them and projected nil at grouping; the\\n  stricter refusal was chosen over an explicit laxness note.\\n\\n## E14 — Syntax depth is bounded by a documented constant\\n\\nRecursive validation, translation and pull parsing walk query syntax by host\\nrecursion. The adapter therefore refuses native query syntax (the query map or\\n- `count`\\n- `count-distinct`\\n- `sum`\\n- `min`\\n- `max`\\n- `avg`\\n\\nAdd:\\n\\n- `distinct`\\n- `median`\\n- `variance`\\n- `stddev`\\n- Parameterized `(min n ?x)` and `(max n ?x)`\\n\\nAggregate parsing must preserve parameters and validate arity. These\\nadditions belong to generic core semantics, not adapter-supplied function\\nclosures.\\n\\nFreeze empty-input results, grouping, duplicate elimination, `:with`\\nmultiplicity, variance convention, median behavior, numeric result types\\nand ordering/tie behavior through fixtures. Arithmetic carriers that the\\nhost cannot safely evaluate remain explicit refusals. Cross-host\\nfloating-point tolerances must be justified per case; do not use a\\nblanket tolerance to conceal type or algorithm differences.\\n\\n`rand`, `sample`, fulltext, database-function lookup and arbitrary custom\\naggregate resolution are excluded. Ambient randomness and global symbol\\nresolution are not acceptable shortcuts. A future random aggregate\\nrequires explicit entropy ownership and consumption semantics.\\n\\nExplicit caller function capabilities remain a DaoSpace extension.\\n(require '[datomic.client.api :as d])\\n(def cl (d/client {:server-type :datomic-local :storage-dir :mem :system \\\"probe\\\"}))\\n(d/create-database cl {:db-name \\\"p\\\"})\\n(def db (d/db (d/connect cl {:db-name \\\"p\\\"})))\\n(defn q* [find vs]\\n  (try (d/q {:query (list :find find :in '$ '[?v ...]) :args [db vs]})\\n       (catch Throwable e (ex-message e))))\\n(doseq [vs [[1 2] [3 4] [1 2 3 4] [37 40] [1.0 2.0] [1 2.0] [-3 -4] [1 2 3]]]\\n  (println vs \\\"median ->\\\" (pr-str (q* '(median ?v) vs))))\\n(println \\\"min 0 ->\\\" (pr-str (q* '(min 0 ?v) [3 1 2])))\\n(println \\\"min -1 ->\\\" (pr-str (q* '(min -1 ?v) [3 1 2])))\\n(println \\\"rand ->\\\" (pr-str (q* '(rand ?v) [3 1 2])))\\n(require '[clojure.walk :as walk]\\n         '[dao.space.datomic-query-fixtures :as fx]\\n         '[dao.space.datomic-query-conformance-test :as ct]\\n         '[dao.space.datomic-query-oracle :as o])\\n(def same? @#'ct/same?)\\n(def load-fixture! @#'o/load-fixture!)\\n(def normalize @#'o/normalize)\\n(def cfg (assoc o/datomic-local-backend :ref-attrs #{:e :person/friend :note/session}))\\n(defn client-fn [s] (requiring-resolve (symbol \\\"datomic.client.api\\\" (name s))))\\n(def cl ((client-fn 'client) (:client cfg)))\\n(defn load-graph!\\n  \\\"Graph nodes that only appear as values need to exist as entities: give\\n   every node a :node/id so the tempid is asserted as an entity too.\\\"\\n  [cl db-name fixture-id]\\n  (let [facts (fx/fixture-facts fixture-id)\\n        nodes (distinct (mapcat (fn [[a _ b]] [a b]) facts))\\n        tid (fn [n] (str \\\"n\\\" n))]\\n    ((client-fn 'create-database) cl {:db-name db-name})\\n    (let [conn ((client-fn 'connect) cl {:db-name db-name})]\\n      ((client-fn 'transact) conn\\n       {:tx-data [{:db/ident :e :db/valueType :db.type/ref :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}\\n                  {:db/ident :node/id :db/valueType :db.type/long :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}]})\\n      (let [{:keys [tempids]} ((client-fn 'transact) conn\\n                               {:tx-data (into (mapv (fn [n] [:db/add (tid n) :node/id n]) nodes)\\n                                               (map (fn [[a _ b]] [:db/add (tid a) :e (tid b)]) facts))})]\\n        {:conn conn\\n         :logical->real (into {} (map (fn [n] [n (get tempids (tid n))])) nodes)}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-case [id]\\n  (let [c (fx/case-by-id id)\\n        fids (vec (distinct (keep #(get % \\\"db\\\") (get c \\\"inputs\\\"))))\\n        names (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i f] [f (str \\\"recon-\\\" id \\\"-\\\" i)])) fids)\\n        dbs (into {} (map (fn [f] [f (if (= f \\\"graph\\\")\\n                                                (load-graph! cl (get names f) f)\\n                                                (load-fixture! cl (get names f) f (:ref-attrs cfg)))])) fids)\\n        l->r (apply merge (map :logical->real (vals dbs)))\\n        r->l (into {} (map (fn [[l r]] [r l])) l->r)\\n        sub (fn [x] (walk/postwalk #(if (and (integer? %) (contains? l->r %)) (get l->r %) %) x))\\n        args (mapv (fn [in] (if (contains? in \\\"db\\\")\\n                              ((client-fn 'db) (get-in dbs [(get in \\\"db\\\") :conn]))\\n                              (sub (fx/decode (get in \\\"value\\\")))))\\n                   (get c \\\"inputs\\\"))\\n        res (try (let [raw ((client-fn 'q) {:query (sub (fx/case-query c)) :args args})]\\n                   [:result (normalize r->l raw)])\\n                 (catch Throwable e [:error (str (class e)) (ex-message e)]))]\\n    (doseq [f fids] (try ((client-fn 'delete-database) cl {:db-name (get names f)}) (catch Throwable _ nil)))\\n    (let [exp (fx/case-expected c)\\n          verdict (when (= :result (first res))\\n                    (if (same? (get c \\\"comparison\\\") exp (set (second res))) :AGREE :DIFFER))]\\n      (println id verdict)\\n      (when (not= verdict :AGREE)\\n        (println \\\"  exp\\\" (pr-str exp))\\n        (println \\\"  obs\\\" (pr-str res))))))\\n(doseq [id *command-line-args*] (run-case id))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./archive/dao-space-query/1792857589000-qa-engineer-datomic-q-phase1.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log:49:- **Tests do not exercise the indexed path directly.** The padding tests assert behaviour (selective constant-entity queries over a plain fact relation and over a `current` view), not that the index is used.\n./archive/yin-vm-wasm/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:56:No collection runs while wasm holds unreported local values. Initially collect only after a full materialization at an exit; later dirty-slot spilling can replace write-through with a separate GC/inspection barrier. Mark active registers, frames, value, continuation, parked/wait/ready entries, stores, module stores, engine heap and pinned/outgoing values, constants, and pending mailbox arguments. Traverse trusted wrappers via `values/payload` and preserve engine `gc-children` treatment of code. Integrate `engine/pin-refs` for arguments leaving through FFI. Keep JS arena ownership and the existing logical heap GC distinct, with explicit roots connecting them. Reclaim only unreachable implementation storage, not stream history or persistent datoms. Avoid handle reuse initially; later reuse needs generations checked at every host import.\n./archive/yin-vm-wasm/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:68:| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\n./archive/yin-vm-wasm/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:182:4. On correlated response, terminal response-loss handling runs first, then call-result unwraps success/error, parked call-id is removed, and rd or tail return receives the result exactly once. Use constants from `yin.vm` for reserved resources.\n./archive/yin-vm-wasm/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:194:3. Compile/instantiate the new component, reserve directory/table capacity, install its body entries, then publish the new code-layout generation atomically. Prefer component-relative constants plus an instance base offset so the same compiled Module can serve different task layouts. Existing absolute pcs resolve through the body directory; body/closure targets emitted at runtime add the base.\n./archive/yin-vm-wasm/1792329904305-architect-register-wasm-lower-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:219:Implement arena, shadow, body dispatcher, constants/moves/loads/branches, closures, explicit calls/returns/halt, primitive results, store/define/gensym, one-step materialization. Effects/continuation execution remains explicitly unavailable until its slice, rather than silently emulated as a completed wasm feature.\n./archive/web-ui/1789038322103-review-postgraphics-gpu-async-fix.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"c