t\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":470}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792523139670,\"messageId\":\"046dd908-9aa2-4a93-bba0-9daf3cc48539\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_55e42b055ece4913b59a3806\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 470 lines\\nNote: showing lines 1-470. More of the file remains. Use offset=471 to continue.\\n1: # dao.stream.host-boundary: dao.stream wherever the boundary can be an IO action\\n2: \\n3: The governing test is the owner's: **dao.stream is used wherever a host\\n4: boundary can be an IO action; a pure, total, synchronous function on the same\\n5: host may be a direct call.** This document's commissionable scope is that\\n6: rule (§2) and the pure-primitive slices H0-H4 (§8): freeze and allowlist; the\\n7: `dao.host.*` integer, float, number, bytes and text profile; migration of\\n8: every consumer; hash and signature entries; the portable Jing CBOR writer.\\n9: Entropy and scheduling, the durable-storage seam, the parser stage and the\\n10: stream conformance suite are **deferred** to a separate design (§9) and are\\n11: not commissionable here.\\n12: \\n13: Status: design only; nothing commissioned. **Revision r5**, 2026-10-20, by\\n14: the Architect (claude-fable-5-1). **Review status: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\\n15: by a non-Claude architect (gpt-6-astra, 2026-10-20,\\n16: `collab/1792508286000-architect-host-boundary-review-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`)\\n17: for the narrowed scope only, §2 plus H0-H4; H5-H8 (§9) are not signed off\\n18: and remain deferred.** The four mechanical corrections M1-M4 that sign-off\\n19: required were applied in r5 (rationale\\n20: `collab/1792508512000-architect-host-boundary-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`)\\n21: and should be confirmed by a non-Claude reviewer before H0 is dispatched.\\n22: r7 (2026-10-21): reconciled with what slice H0 measured (F1-F4: the 16\\n23: unlisted host arms categorised, the closure-based effect counts, the §3.1\\n24: rows, the print and M-entry facts); rationale\\n25: `collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\\n26: **a non-Claude reviewer must confirm this r7 before H1 is dispatched.**\\n27: r6: D1-D3 applied (a non-Claude reviewer's confirmation check,\\n28: `collab/1792517656000-reviewer-host-boundary-r5.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`,\\n29: found M1-M4 applied and three low defects; rationale\\n30: `collab/1792518203000-architect-host-boundary-r6.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\\n31: Lineage: r1 rationale\\n32: `collab/1792495909000-architect-host-boundary.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\\n33: r1 review NOT SIGNED OFF\\n34: (`collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\\n35: A1-A8); r2 rationale `collab/1792502807000-...-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\\n36: r2 review NOT SIGNED OFF\\n37: (`collab/1792503557000-architect-host-boundary-review-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\\n38: R1-R6); r3 rationale and disposition\\n39: `collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\\n40: r3 scoped review NOT SIGNED OFF\\n41: (`collab/1792507576000-architect-host-boundary-review-r3.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\\n42: N1-N6); **r4** answers N1-N6 within the unchanged scope (rationale\\n43: `collab/1792507856000-architect-host-boundary-r4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\\n44: Owner decision for r3 (verbatim): \\\"yes, proceed with the split\\\". No code was\\n45: changed; every claim about the tree is labelled. **Because this document was\\n46: written by a Claude model, a non-Claude architect must sign off this final\\n47: text (§2 plus H0-H4) before anything is commissioned from it** (§14).\\n48: \\n49: Owner direction, verbatim. Original: \\\"maybe this is an opportunity to stop\\n50: and refactor so that the dao.stream is the boundary that crosses over host\\n51: specific dependency. once that is done, the only thing needs porting is\\n52: dao.stream implementations\\\". Refined: \\\"maybe my dao.stream ABI framing has to\\n53: be refined.  when a host-boundary is cross should it be used. when its on the\\n54: same host, then direct function call is fine. In other words, when the host\\n55: boundary can be an IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad\\n56: than dao.stream doesnt have to be used\\\". Asked which formulation governs:\\n57: \\\"yes, test 2 governs\\\".\\n58: \\n59: Authorities:\\n60: - [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) `:43-48`, `:70`.\\n61: - [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §3 custody; §4 machine-internal\\n62:   work executes inside the instance; §5 the floor and internal linkage; §6\\n63:   effects out, events in; §7 adapter discipline (`:143-148`). §12 is an\\n64:   owner proposal to amend it; nothing here edits it.\\n65: - [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the contract; cursors are non-destructive\\n66:   (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159-173`, V via the r2 review).\\n67: - [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §1 `:29-52`: seven imports.\\n68: - [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md): the codec track; untouched\\n69:   here (§8.3).\\n70: - [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5; [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md);\\n71:   [dao.jing.cbor.md](./dao.jing.cbor.md).\\n72: \\n73: Labels: V = verified by reading this tree on 2026-10-20 (line cites); R =\\n74: read, computed by script, or inferred, not run under a compiler; P =\\n75: proposed. Nothing was executed on a lane.\\n76: \\n77: Standalone: a future host lane is an example consumer, never a dependency.\\n78: \\n79: ---\\n80: \\n81: ## 1. Problem\\n82: \\n83: V: the portable core (`src/cljc`, 183 files) reaches hosts two ways and the\\n84: doctrine sanctions only one. The sanctioned way is the transports' pattern:\\n85: a portable core plus a per-host seam value the composition hands in\\n86: (`yin.repl.host/websocket`, `host.cljc:20-37`; `datagram.jvm/bind!`,\\n87: `jvm.clj:22-34`). The other way is host contact inside portable files: reader\\n88: arms reaching a host library, and resolved calls to host-backed core\\n89: functions that compile everywhere and still touch the world.\\n90: \\n91: The in-scope files (V, `rg -c '#\\\\?'`; what the arms reach):\\n92: \\n93: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n94: | File                              | `#?`  | Host contact (V)                                                                                        |\\n95: +===================================+=======+=========================================================================================================+\\n96: | `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (1562 lines) | 52    | Big-integer helpers (`:106-254`); `Float64`/`Rational`/`Decimal` carriers (`:342-460`); JVM             |\\n97: |                                   |       | `BigDecimal`/`Ratio` construction and access (`:483-632`); float bits (`:525-541`); writer selection    |\\n98: |                                   |       | (`:58-60`: Boring on JVM and JS, the Dart `cbor` package).                                              |\\n99: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n100: | `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (299)  | 30    | 25 public functions (`:41-298`): carrier recognition, promotion, 13 big operations.                     |\\n101: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n102: | `dao/jing.cljc` (629)             | 12    | BLAKE3 and SHA-256 libraries (`:32-40, :59-135`); an opaque sentinel (`:43-51`).                        |\\n103: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n104: | `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (274)   | 16    | Ed25519 libraries; `jvm-pair` replays a given seed through a `SecureRandom` proxy (`:75-87`, V:         |\\n105: |                                   |       | deterministic, not fresh entropy); `generate` draws fresh entropy (`:114-124`, deferred, §9).           |\\n106: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n107: | `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`       | 14    | The two wire writers (`:cljd nil`-gated Boring wrapper; 107-line Dart twin).                            |\\n108: | (132), `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cl |       |                                                                                                         |\\n109: | jd.cljd`                          |       |                                                                                                         |\\n110: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n111: | `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` (16)       | 16    | Bytes predicate (`:167`), char access (`:241`), NFC (`:310-321`, Unicode 16.0 owner-settled), float     |\\n112: |                                   |       | bits (`:855-885`), carrier tests (`:1255-1257`).                                                        |\\n113: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n114: | `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` (13)  | 13    | Float64 little-endian hex (`:232-242`); JVM big-integer predicate (`:315-317`).                         |\\n115: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n116: | `yin/vm/data.cljc` (7),           | 7+9   | Code units and `units->string` (`:119-173`); `integral` (`:77-92`); `Closure`/`Continuation` equality,  |\\n117: | `yin/vm/values.cljc` (9)          |       | hash and print arms (`:39-119`).                                                                        |\\n118: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n119: | `dao/stream/chunks.cljc` (5),     | 5+4+4 | `Uint8List` length, alloc, slice, copy (`chunks.cljc:8-32`); base64 libraries; host-bytes predicate     |\\n120: | `base64.cljc`, `codec.cljc`       |       | (`codec.cljc:45`, codec-track file, §8.3).                                                              |\\n121: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n122: | `dao/jing/dht.cljc` (10),         | 10+14 | Bytes helpers (`dht.cljc:100-121`); bytes length (`linker.cljc:1232`); `host-bytes`/`utf8`              |\\n123: | `yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `datagram`  |       | (`sign.cljc:55-66`); byte length (`datagram.cljc:63-66`).                                               |\\n124: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n125: | `dao/data/arrays.cljc` (21)       | 21    | Host arrays, `^long` hints, `parallelSort` (`:145`).                                                    |\\n126: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n127: \\n128: Out of scope here and listed in §9: `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/\\n129: fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`, `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`,\\n130: `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (storage); `dao/jing/content/async.cljc`\\n131: (outside the closure, H0; recorded for the deferred design),\\n132: `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:80` and `src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj`\\n133: (scheduling, blocking driver); every entropy site; `yang/python/antlr/\\n134: parser.cljc` and the `yang.antlr.cst` hosts (parser stage).\\n135: \\n136: V, the duplication: `yin.vm.integer.host` says it \\\"does not reach into\\n137: `dao.jing.cbor`'s private big-integer helpers\\\" (`host.cljc:23-25`); `cbor.cljc`\\n138: keeps its own (`:112-216`). Two shims, one contract.\\n139: \\n140: ## 2. The governing rule\\n141: \\n142: ### 2.1 The boundary, defined first (review R1)\\n143: \\n144: **The machine** is the running instance and the state it owns: its values,\\n145: and the mutable runtime state its own code created and no other party can\\n146: observe or change: atoms and volatiles, host arrays and byte arrays it\\n147: allocated, a B-tree's weak or soft references, the collector that clears\\n148: them, thread-local locks between threads the driver created. **The world** is\\n149: everything else: files, sockets, other processes, the operating system's\\n150: clock, entropy sources, environment, the terminal, and any other instance.\\n151: \\n152: A host contact is **external** when it observes or changes the world. It is\\n153: **internal** when it is a function of the machine's values and owned runtime\\n154: state only.\\n155: \\n156: ### 2.2 Statement\\n157: \\n158: **An external contact uses `dao.stream`: it can be an IO action. An internal\\n159: contact that is a pure, total, synchronous function of its arguments, or a\\n160: declared mutation of an owned argument (§2.4), may be a direct call and is\\n161: then an entry of the primitive profile (§4.3).**\\n162: \\n163: This is the owner's test 2. Same-host-ness is not the criterion: a same-host\\n164: file read is external and is a stream; a hash from a library linked into the\\n165: instance is internal and is a direct call.\\n166: \\n167: ### 2.3 \\\"Can be an IO action\\\", operationally\\n168: \\n169: A contact can be an IO action when any of these holds **with respect to the\\n170: world** (§2.1):\\n171: \\n172: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n173: | #  | Property                       | Meaning and the doctrine that names it                                                                     |\\n174: +====+================================+============================================================================================================+\\n175: | 1  | Observation of the world       | The result depends on world state: a file, a socket, another process, the environment, a resource's        |\\n176: |    |                                | current condition. `abi.md` §5: \\\"world facts that shipped code cannot compute\\\".                            |\\n177: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n178: | 2  | Effect on the world            | Calling it changes world state: bytes on disk, a packet sent, a lock taken, text printed.                  |\\n179: |    |                                | `datom.world.md:46`: \\\"Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\\\"                                       |\\n180: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n181: | 3  | World nondeterminism           | Two calls with equal arguments and equal owned state may answer differently because of the world: entropy, |\\n182: |    |                                | a clock, an OS-assigned pid, a process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own.             |\\n183: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n184: | 4  | Asynchrony                     | The answer is not available at return, or completion invokes something later: a timer, a thread, a         |\\n185: |    |                                | promise. `dao.stream.md`: \\\"No operation waits\\\"; `abi.md` §6: a callback is an event on a stream.           |\\n186: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n187: | 5  | World-dependent failure        | Whether it completes depends on the world (disk full, peer gone), not on the input. `dao.stream.md` Result |\\n188: |    |                                | Convention: such outcomes are data.                                                                        |\\n189: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n190: | 6  | Another process or host        | The work runs in a different process, machine or trust domain. `abi.md` §3.                                |\\n191: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n192: \\n193: A contact cannot be an IO action when none holds. Then it is a function of\\n194: the machine's values and owned state, computed synchronously in the same\\n195: instance, whose only failures are functions of the input, answered as data\\n196: or a refusal. Linking a library to compute it is internal linkage (`abi.md`\\n197: §5 `:80-81`); executing it is machine-internal work (`abi.md` §4 `:60-62`).\\n198: The basis of the primitive profile is therefore **internality plus purity,\\n199: totality and synchrony over the declared domain**, with mutation admitted\\n200: only as §2.4 declares it.\\n201: \\n202: A read of owned runtime state that may differ between calls (a weak\\n203: reference after a collection, an atom another owned thread advanced) fails\\n204: none of the six properties: it is a fact about the machine, not the world.\\n205: It is a **runtime prerequisite** (table C), neither IO nor a profile entry.\\n206: r2 classified it as both; that contradiction is withdrawn.\\n207: \\n208: ### 2.4 Ownership and mutation laws for the profile\\n209: \\n210: The profile's purity claim is exact, not blanket:\\n211: \\n212: - **Pure entries** (marked P in table A) are deterministic, total over their\\n213:   declared domain, synchronous, and allocate only fresh values; equal\\n214:   arguments give equal results on every host.\\n215: - **Mutating entries** (marked M: `bytes/set!`, `bytes/copy!`, `arrays/aset`,\\n216:   `arrays/acopy`, `arrays/asort`) mutate **exactly the argument named as the\\n217:   target** and nothing else, and never retain it. Return values are per\\n218:   entry, not uniform: `aset` returns the assigned value (Clojure's `aset`\\n219:   semantics, V `arrays.cljc:50-57`, unchanged); `set!`, `copy!` and `acopy`\\n220:   return nil; `asort` returns the sorted array. Overlapping `copy!` and\\n221:   `acopy` behave as memmove (V: `acopy` already handles same-array overlap,\\n222:   `:97-105`). **Prevalidation**: every index and length is checked before\\n223:   any write, so a refused call leaves the target byte-identical. This is a\\n224:   **behaviour change** on JS and Dart, where today's `acopy` loop writes\\n225:   element by element and can write before meeting an invalid index\\n226:   (`arrays.cljc:97-105`, V; the JVM's `System/arraycopy` checks first); it\\n227:   is assigned to H2 with a no-partial-write test (table D).\\n228: - **Aliasing**: `bytes/slice`, `arrays/asub`, `arrays/aclone`, `from-vector`\\n229:   always return a fresh value; no entry returns a view onto an argument.\\n230:   Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\\n231:   **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\\n232:   object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\\n233:   ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\\n234:   (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\\n235:   possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\\n236: - **Exclusive mutation right, ownership transfer, and freezing\\n237:   publication.** A mutable profile value (bytes, a host array) has exactly\\n238:   one party with the right to mutate it. Two distinct events move or end\\n239:   that right:\\n240:   - **Ownership transfer** (the right moves, the value stays mutable): when\\n241:     a profile entry marked \\\"fresh\\\" (`alloc`, `slice`, `from-vector`,\\n242:     `magnitude-bytes`, `anew`, `aclone`, `asub`, `aconcat`, `ainsert-at`,\\n243:     `aremove-at`, `from-coll`, `utf8-encode`) returns a value, the **caller**\\n244:     holds the exclusive mutation right from that return, and may `set!`,\\n245:     `copy!`, `aset`, `acopy` or `asort` it. Returning fresh storage is not\\n246:     publication.\\n247:   - **Freezing publication** (the right ends for everyone): appending the\\n248:     value to a stream (in-memory or not); placing it inside a value that is\\n249:     appended; handing it to a seam or a content handle. On publication the\\n250:     value is **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it\\n251:     afterwards, and a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is\\n252:     transitive: a collection is frozen with every mutable value nested in\\n253:     it. A party that needs a mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`,\\n254:     `aclone` or `from-vector`, and then holds the right to that copy.\\n255: - **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\\n256:   argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\\n257:   text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\\n258:   named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\\n259:   (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\\n260:   fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\\n261:   the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\\n262:   the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule.\\n263:   **What the H0 gate can and cannot see (V, H0 report).** The gate resolves\\n264:   a call as an M entry only when it goes through `dao.data.arrays` or\\n265:   `dao.host.bytes` by alias; today that is `dao.data.btree` alone (33 calls,\\n266:   owned node arrays, never appended). `dao.stream.chunks` (`absorb`,\\n267:   `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor` (`magnitude-bytes` and the\\n268:   byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`) and `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`)\\n269:   mutate host arrays **directly inside reader arms** (`System/arraycopy`,\\n270:   `.setRange`, `.set`, `aset`) and call no M entry until H2 migrates them,\\n271:   so the gate cannot count them now. Obligations (i)-(iii) are tests, not\\n272:   gate checks; the freeze rule itself (no mutation after publication) is\\n273:   not mechanically checkable by the gate at all and remains a review\\n274:   obligation. **What H2's audit must do instead**: (a) list every direct\\n275:   host-array mutation inside an arm across the closure (the gate's arm\\n276:   scan plus a search for `arraycopy`, `setRange`, `.set`, `aset`, `aclone`\\n277:   on byte arrays); (b) replace each with an M entry or a fresh-allocating P\\n278:   entry, so that after H2 the only mutation sites in the closure are M-entry\\n279:   calls; (c) add the migrated callers to list 2 as `:m-entry` callers with\\n280:   their ceilings and a one-line reason each; (d) record any site that keeps\\n281:   a direct mutation (expected: none) as a named exception. After H2 a new\\n282:   M-entry caller fails the gate's list 2 until reviewed; before H2 the gate\\n283:   catches only new `dao.data.arrays` callers.\\n284:   What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\\n285:   obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\\n286:   rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\\n287: \\n288: ### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\\n289: \\n290: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n291: | Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\\n292: +===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\\n293: | UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,         | Direct     |\\n294: | decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile    | call       |\\n295: |                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\\n296: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n297: | Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same    | Direct     |\\n298: | a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\\n299: | Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                           | instance   |\\n300: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n301: | Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                    | Stream;    |\\n302: | generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\\n303: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n304: | Time; scheduling; the JVM         | Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps | Stream /   |\\n305: | blocking content driver           | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\\n306: |                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\\n307: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n308: | The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.  | Either;    |\\n309: |                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.  | deferred   |\\n310: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n311: | Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                           | Stream;    |\\n312: | rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\\n313: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n314: | Weak and soft references,         | Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode   | Runtime    |\\n315: | atomics, `locking`, atoms         | is `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent   | prereq.    |\\n316: |                                   | of the mode, only retention differs.                                                                    |            |\\n317: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n318: | Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard | Dialect    |\\n319: | `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,    | semantics  |\\n320: |                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,  |            |\\n321: |                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.               |            |\\n322: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n323: | `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred   | Deferred   |\\n324: |                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\\n325: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\\n326: \\n327: ### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\\n328: \\n329: Met for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\\n330: host's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\\n331: a port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\\n332: dialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\\n333: profile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\\n334: a design.\\n335: \\n336: ## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\\n337: \\n338: ### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\\n339: \\n340: Method: a require-form traversal from `yin.repl.main`, choosing each lane's\\n341: reader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`) and resolving each namespace to its\\n342: file under `src/<lane>` first (shadow resolution: `yin.repl.host` is\\n343: `host.cljc` on the JVM and the `host.cljs`/`host.cljd` shadows elsewhere),\\n344: then `src/cljc`. It is not a compiler-resolved call graph: it can\\n345: over-include a namespace required but unused on a path, cannot see dynamic\\n346: loading, and **can under-include** when its reader-arm parsing fails; the\\n347: r3 script did exactly that (it dropped requires inside `#?@` arms and\\n348: reported 141/140/139). The r3 claim that it \\\"cannot under-include\\\" is\\n349: withdrawn. The reconciled totals, counted by **file** and confirmed by the\\n350: r3 reviewer's independent traversal (R):\\n351: \\n352: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n353: | Lane  | Total | Shared   | Lane-specific files (V, the files exist)                                                                            |\\n354: +=======+=======+==========+=====================================================================================================================+\\n355: | clj   | 142   | 133      | `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj` (blocking driver: clock, sleep, `locking`), `clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj`,    |\\n356: |       |       |          | `clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`, `clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj` (clock, `CompletableFuture`), `clj/yang/antlr/     |\\n357: |       |       |          | cst.clj`, `clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`, `cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc` (the JVM selector),                                |\\n358: |       |       |          | `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Nine.                                         |\\n359: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n360: | cljs  | 140   | 133      | `cljs/dao/stream/datagram/node.cljs`, `cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs` (`setTimeout`), `cljs/dao/stream/ws/    |\\n361: |       |       |          | node.cljs` (`js/Date`), `cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs`, `cljs/yin/repl/host.cljs` (shadow), `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/        |\\n362: |       |       |          | boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Seven.                                                            |\\n363: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n364: | cljd  | 141   | 133      | `cljd/dao/stream/cbor/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/transit/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/datagram/dart.cljd`,        |\\n365: |       |       |          | `cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd` (`Timer`), `cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd` (`DateTime.now`), `cljd/yang/antlr/ |\\n366: |       |       |          | cst.cljd`, `cljd/yin/repl/host.cljd` (shadow), `cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`. Eight.                               |\\n367: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n368: \\n369: The 133 shared files are `.cljc`. H0 commits the reproducible evidence\\n370: (§8): the traversal is re-implemented over `clojure.tools.namespace`'s\\n371: `ns` parsing (which handles reader conditionals per platform) rather than a\\n372: regex, committed as `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0 placed it\\n373: under `src/dev/` with the repo's other dev tools), and its output as\\n374: `docs/design/host-boundary/closure-{clj,cljs,cljd}.edn`; H0's acceptance is\\n375: that the committed output lists exactly the 142/140/141 files above, and\\n376: that the two traversals (the committed tool and the r3 reviewer's method)\\n377: agree, recorded in the slice report. Until that lands, the totals here are\\n378: R.\\n379: \\n380: Classification of every file in the closure that has any host contact (the\\n381: remaining files have none beyond §3.3 syntax; the committed H0 output marks\\n382: each file with its class, which is what makes \\\"remaining\\\" checkable):\\n383: \\n384: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n385: | Namespaces                                          | Contact (V)                            | Class and slice                                          |\\n386: +=====================================================+========================================+==========================================================+\\n387: | `yin.vm.integer.host`, `dao.jing.cbor` (helpers,    | Big integers, floats, Decimal/Ratio,   | Internal pure: profile. H1-H2 (writer: H4).              |\\n388: | carriers, Decimal/Ratio, float bits, writer),       | NFC, bytes, code units, host arrays    |                                                          |\\n389: | `dao.jing.cbor.boring`, `dao.jing.cbor.cljd`,       |                                        |                                                          |\\n390: | `yin.vm.debruijn`, `yin.vm.debruijn-code`,          |                                        |                                                          |\\n391: | `yin.vm.data`, `yin.vm.values`,                     |                                        |                                                          |\\n392: | `dao.stream.chunks`, `dao.stream.base64`,           |                                        |                                                          |\\n393: | `dao.jing.dht` (bytes), `yin.vm.linker` (`:1232`),  |                                        |                                                          |\\n394: | `dao.stream.datagram` (`:63-66`), `dao.data.arrays` |                                        |                                                          |\\n395: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n396: | `dao.jing` (hashes), `yin.vm.linker.sign`           | Hash and signature libraries; seed     | Internal pure, in-instance: profile. H3. `generate` is   |\\n397: | (`public-of`, `sign-bytes`, verify, `jvm-pair`)     | replay                                 | entropy: deferred.                                       |\\n398: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n399: | `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.values`,         | Require `yin.vm.integer.host` for      | Consumers: H2 (require change only).                     |\\n400: | `yin.vm.data`, `yin.vm.integer`                     | `big-carrier?`/`exact-integer?`        |                                                          |\\n401: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n402: | `dao.stream.ws`, `.datagram`, the seams             | Sockets; wake sources (`setTimeout`,   | External: streams with seams (existing). No slice. The   |\\n403: | (`ws.jvm/node/dart`, `datagram.jvm/node/dart`), the | `Timer`, a blocking queue); the ws     | ws seams also read a clock (`ws/jvm.clj:422`,            |\\n404: | three `dao.stream.waitset.driver` files,            | seams' clock reads                     | `ws/node.cljs:249`, `ws/dart.cljd:176`, V): seam-private |\\n405: | `yin.repl.host*`; `dao.stream.ringbuffer:168` (a    |                                        | IO, allowed by §4.1. (`dao.stream.udp` and the browser   |\\n406: | host atom type test inside the in-memory            |                                        | adapter are not in the closure, H0.)                     |\\n407: | implementation, V)                                  |                                        |                                                          |\\n408: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n409: | `dao.jing.file`, `dao.space.store.fs`,              | Files, locks, rename, dir sync, pid,   | External: storage seam. **Deferred** (§9.2).             |\\n410: | `dao.space.store`, `dao.stream.journal.file`,       | platform                               |                                                          |\\n411: | `dao.data.btree.storage` (promise facade)           |                                        |                                                          |\\n412: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\\n413: | 21 `random-uuid` sites in the closure (§3.2),   